# The Weaver of Dawn

In the time before time, darkness covered the world like a heavy blanket. The people stumbled through endless night, guided only by touch and memory.

Among them lived Nalani, a young weaver whose fingers could create patterns unseen. Though blind from birth, she claimed to see colors in her dreams—brilliant reds, warm golds, soft purples that danced and swirled.

One night, Nalani dreamed of the most magnificent color yet: a radiant yellow-white that filled her with inexplicable warmth. When she awoke, her fingers moved frantically across her loom, weaving threads of gold and silver, amber and pearl.

For seven days and nights she wove, never eating, never resting. The elders grew concerned, but none could stop her fevered work. On the eighth day, she cut the cloth from her loom and held it high.

The fabric blazed with light, burning away the darkness. It rose from her hands, unfurling across the sky, and became the sun.

Nalani fell into deep sleep, her purpose fulfilled. When she finally awoke, she could see—not just colors, but the world illuminated by her creation.

They say weavers still carry her gift, creating beauty from mere thread and dream.
