Myth: In the time before seasons, the Earth spun beneath an unchanging sky. Days were all alike—neither warm nor cold, neither bright nor dim—and the world grew weary of sameness.

The Earth called out to the Sky, "Why do you never change? I long for variation, for rhythm, for something new."

The Sky replied softly, "I do change, but you cannot see it from where you stand."

Curious, the Earth tilted herself, leaning gently to one side as she circled the Sun. Suddenly, the Sky's light fell upon her differently. Where the tilt brought her closer, warmth bloomed and flowers opened—this became Summer. Where she leaned away, cold descended and snow fell—this became Winter.

Between these extremes, she discovered Spring's gentle awakening and Autumn's graceful release.

"Sky, you were right!" the Earth exclaimed. "The change was always there. I only needed to shift my perspective to see it."

The Sky smiled, stars twinkling. "Sometimes transformation requires not that the world changes, but that we change how we meet it."

